C = [
("h1", "3. “By One Man Sin Entered”"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:12–14 says four things about the first man and the world, says a fifth thing about the law, and then stops in the middle of its sentence. A reader who reports exactly those things, and no more, has the passage that Paul wrote. A good deal of the history of the passage is a history of adding to them."),
("sub", "3.1 The sentence that does not finish"),
("p", "The King James Version prints verse 12 as a sentence that begins with “as” and never reaches “so.” “Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned” (Romans 5:12). The “and so” in the middle is not the answer to the “as.” It belongs to the first half of the comparison, and it says that death followed sin in the way it entered. The second half of the comparison, the part that would say how the gift came by the other man, is not written here."),
("p", "Paul does not write it because he breaks off. The King James text puts verses 13 to 17 in a parenthesis, and the comparison is taken up again with “Therefore as by the offence of one judgment came upon all men to condemnation; even so by the righteousness of one the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life” (Romans 5:18). The distance between verse 12 and verse 18 is the distance of a long aside. In the aside Paul answers a question that his own opening sentence raises: if sin entered by one man, what was the position of men before the law?"),
("sub", "3.2 Verse 12 clause by clause"),
("ctbl", "What Romans 5:12 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“by one man sin entered into the world”", "Sin came into the world by one man", "How sin was passed from him to others; the text says it entered the world"),
    ("“and death by sin”", "Death came by sin", "Whether death is the only result of sin"),
    ("“and so death passed upon all men”", "Death passed to all men, in the way it had entered", "The mechanism of the passing"),
    ("“for that all have sinned”", "All men have sinned, and this is given as the ground on which death reaches them", "Whether “all have sinned” means all sinned in the first man’s act or each in his own; the sentence does not say"),
  ], [0.28, 0.36, 0.36]),
("p", "Four things are said. One man; sin entering the world by him; death following by sin; death reaching all men on the ground that all have sinned. The fourth clause is the one on which the whole history of the passage turns, and the next chapters come back to it. For now the point is only this: the clause names “all” as the ones who have sinned, and it gives their sinning as the reason death passed to them. It does not say in what sense they sinned. The sentences that follow say something about that, and the volume reads them before it says more."),
("sub", "3.3 Sin in the world before the law (5:13)"),
("p", "“(For until the law sin was in the world: but sin is not imputed when there is no law” (Romans 5:13). Two statements stand side by side. Sin was in the world before the law was given; and sin is not imputed where there is no law. The first prevents any reader from thinking that the world was sinless until Moses. The second says that sin is not reckoned in the same way where there is no law. Paul uses the same word in the same sense elsewhere in the letter, where he says that “by the law is the knowledge of sin” (Romans 3:20), and where he says of the man whose faith is counted that God “imputeth righteousness without works” (Romans 4:6)."),
("p", "The verse touches a question that is the subject of another book from the same press. Study 59, The Law’s Jurisdiction and the Body of Christ, asks to whom the law was given and what jurisdiction it held. This volume does not repeat that study. It reports what Romans 5:13 says: sin was in the world, and imputing sin belongs to the presence of law. It does not go on to say what the law covered, whom it covered, or where its reach ended. Those are the subject of the study."),
("sub", "3.4 Death reigned (5:14)"),
("p", "“Nevertheless death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over them that had not sinned after the similitude of Adam’s transgression, who is the figure of him that was to come” (Romans 5:14). The verse has three parts. First, death reigned, and the verb is the verb of a king. Second, it reigned over a span of time: “from Adam to Moses.” Third, it reigned over a class of men who are described by what they had not done: they “had not sinned after the similitude of Adam’s transgression.”"),
("p", "The class is not named. The text does not say that it was infants, or any named group of persons, or the whole of the world between Adam and Moses. It says what the members of the class had not done. They had not sinned in the way Adam sinned, which the text calls a “transgression,” the breaking of a commandment given directly. They had sinned, as verse 13 says, and they had died, as verse 14 says, but they had not transgressed as he had. The verse is the evidence for what verse 13 says: sin and death were in the world before the law, and men died whether or not they had broken a command like Adam’s."),
("tbl", "Romans 5:12–14, verse by verse.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:12", "“as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin”", "Two entrances: sin by the man, death by sin"),
    ("Rom 5:12", "“so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned”", "Death reaches all, and the ground given is that all have sinned"),
    ("Rom 5:13", "“until the law sin was in the world: but sin is not imputed when there is no law”", "Sin was present; its imputing belongs with law"),
    ("Rom 5:14", "“death reigned from Adam to Moses”", "A period marked by two names"),
    ("Rom 5:14", "“even over them that had not sinned after the similitude of Adam’s transgression”", "A class described by what it had not done"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "3.5 What the volume takes from the three verses"),
("p", "The volume takes four statements and no more. Sin entered the world by one man. Death came by sin and reached all men. Sin was in the world before the law, and the imputing of it belongs with law. Death reigned between Adam and Moses even over men who had not transgressed as Adam did. These are the statements Paul makes. A reader who wishes to build a fuller doctrine of how Adam’s act is related to each person’s condition will find several such doctrines on offer, and the objections chapter gives them with their holders’ names. The text itself does not choose among them in these three verses, and the volume does not choose for it."),
("p", "There is a gain in holding to the narrower report. It lets the later verses of the passage say what they say. In verses 15 to 19 Paul says a good deal more about the one act and its results, and a reader who has not decided in advance what verse 12 means is better placed to hear it."),
]
